less you know you  need more than that for your specific  use case for number four some people  might assume that this new Macbook Pro  comes with other feature updates but no  the design is identical the display is  identical the webcam is the same the  speakers are the same the port layout  everything all the changes are on the  inside with the only other difference  being a new space gray braided cable to  match that model and the new HDMI 2.0  Port which supports 8K displays or 4K at  240 hertz now for number five if you  care a lot about fast charging then  don't buy the base M2 Pro 14 inch model  because it comes with a slower 67 watt  power adapter by simply upgrading the  chip you'll automatically get the 96  watt fast charger which will charge it  up to 50 percent in 30 minutes however  every single 16 inch model comes with  the crazy good 140 watt power adapter  for 50 charge and 30 minutes even on the  much larger 100 watt hour battery so  keep that point in mind for number six  you might be tempted to upgrade to a new  Macbook Pro for the new Wi-Fi 6E and  Bluetooth 5.3 specs but honestly it's  not going to make that big of a  difference especially since Wi-Fi 60  support on the MacBook requires a Wi-Fi  6E router and they're still very  expensive so while it's a nice spec  upgrade don't put too much weight on it  in terms of your decision to upgrade or  to buy for number seven don't  underestimate the 14 inch M2 Max model  because it comes with something very  special this year if you didn't already  know the 16 inch M1 Max model from 2021  actually weighed a little bit more than  the M1 Pro chip model and that's because  they beefed up the cooling system and  the heat sink but they unfortunately  didn't do that for the 14 inch M1 Max  but this time around the 14 inch M2 Max  model is actually heavier than the M2  Pro hinting that Apple probably improved  the cooling system to fight overheating  and thermal throttling which was a big  issue on the M1 Max 14 inch so subscribe  right now if you want to see us tear it  down to find out now for number eight  please do not buy the 8 terabyte SSD  model it's absolutely overkill for a lot  of people out there I've had a one  terabyte 16 inch for over two years and  it's been completely fine for me so I  would suggest that you stick to either a  one terabyte or maybe two terabyte  storage model don't waste money going  all out because it's going to lose its  resale value insanely fast especially  after Apple updates the MacBook Pro  again and also you never know if you're  going to be tempted into buying the M3  Pro or Max version in about a year and a  half so upgrading to 8 terabytes is a  bad idea for literally everybody out  there now for number nine don't assume  that the 14-inch MacBook Pro is  identical to the 16 inch in terms of  features the 16 inch model actually  comes with a couple of bonuses that you  might not know about of course the  display is larger which is obvious but  the added benefit is that the bezels  actually look slimmer due to the ratios  as well as the notch at the top actually  looking smaller for the same reason on  top of that the 16 inch gets a much  larger trackpad which is more convenient  to use and the speakers are actually a  lot better as well compared to the 14  inch as far as battery life the 16 inch  gets three hours more in terms of web  browsing which is great and don't forget  that the 16 inch gets a more powerful  140 watt power supply which also can be  used to charge other things now for  number 10 this is disclaimer if you  already have an M1 Pro or M1 Max MacBook  Pro do not upgrade I don't think it's  worth it at all sure the CPU and GPU  performance is 20 to 30 percent higher  but if you won't be taking advantage of  the extra performance every day it's not  worth it and I would even say that if  you're gonna have to go into debt to buy  one of these new models don't do it the  economy isn't looking so great right now  and the last thing that you need is more  debt just keep whatever MacBook you have  already until everything stabilizes  again and now let's get into my M2 Pro  and Max MacBook Pro buyer's guide with  my own personal recommendations starting  with a base 14 inch model for two  thousand dollars this is going to be the  one that I recommend for people who  usually buy less expensive laptops in  the Thousand to thirteen hundred dollar  range but have decided to finally go for  the high-end MacBook Pro this time  around and that's a great idea because a  feature is like the 120 hertz promotion  in mini LED display which is fantastic I  honestly think that for the common  person the base model with the bend M2  Pro chip is more than enough for 80  percent of the people out there giving  you the snappiest web browsing  performance you can get and enough  overall performance to handle many of  the common productivity apps like Photo  and 4K video editing now people doing  photo editing should also consider  upgrading to 32 gigs of RAM because it  really helps improve performance and  even regular users who tend to have a  ton of Internet tabs and apps open at  the same time should consider upgrading  to 32 as well to avoid any slowdowns  from completely full memory and as far  as the actual chip upgrades I honestly  don't think they're worth the extra  price unless you do productivity work  every day and especially if whatever  you're doing is making you money and in  that case you can probably write it off  as a business expense as well and in  terms of which chip upgrades to make  keep in mind that the M2 Max chip has  identical CPU performance to the spec'd  up M2 Pro chip there's no CPU  performance difference at all the M2 Max  is really there for the upgraded GPU  since it has double the GPU cores so if  you're a programmer who needs a lot of  CPU performance the M2 Max will do  nothing for you compared to the high-end  Mt Pro do not upgrade to it just simply  upgrade to the spec'd up 12 core CPU and  that's it which will be a must for  serious programmers and others doing CPU  based tasks like photo editing every day  however if you're someone who absolutely  needs more than 32 gigs of RAM like Anna  on Twitter who trains neural networks  and needs 96 gigs in that case Apple  forces you to get the M2 Max to go above  32 gigs of RAM so you're gonna have to  pay for that and on the other hand if  you don't care about CPU performance  like for people doing 3D rendering or  Graphics based video editing you're also  forced to get the 12 core CPU that comes  with the M2 Max but it'll definitely be  worth it since you're getting double the  GPU course and if you're a gamer you  absolutely need to get the 38 core GPU  since graphics performance is everything  for gaming but honestly 32 gigs of RAM  will be just fine for gaming so don't go  above that now if you're a video editor  3D video renderer gamer photo editor or  anything that relies a lot on the  display then I would recommend the  16-inch model because the extra screen  real estate gives you a huge advantage  and makes it so much easier to do your  work and on top of that the 16 inch  model is also great for watching movies  and shows because the display is more  immersive and the speakers actually get  a lot louder and better so it's worth it  so there you have of it there's my  personal buyer's guide for the new  Macbook Pros and if you'd rather get an  M1 Pro 14 inch for only sixteen hundred  dollars you can use the YouTube tagging  feature to check it out so click the  circle about to subscribe and don't  forget to comment to enter the giveaway  thanks for watching and we'll see you in  the next video  [Music]
 

